# zfs-aurora-complex

Read AGENTS.md section 0 first — it is the maintained orientation document.
These rules are the short form and deliberately do not restate it, so there is
one copy to keep current. Documentation here has drifted from the code before.

## Context

An Aurora DX bootc image with ZFS. It builds the ZFS kmod rather than
reusing one: a self-hosted akmods cache image is built from a pinned upstream
fork, and containerfiles/zfs-akmods/install_zfs_from_akmods_cache.py installs
the RPMs for one kernel out of it.

The decision-making is Python, not YAML. Every workflow step that decides
anything runs `python3 -m ci_tools.cli <command>`; .github/scripts/README.md
maps step to command to module.

## Before proposing anything

This publishes a signed :latest that `bootc upgrade` pulls, and there is no
staging tier between a merge and a machine booting it. The maintainer
daily-drives this image on real hardware with multi-terabyte ZFS pools attached,
and other systems track it (docs/safety-model.md). Merging to main triggers the
production workflow, which builds, signs with this repo's key, and promotes.

- A green pipeline is not a good image. It proves the build completed. Say which
  run you actually read.
- "It compiled" is not evidence a ZFS module is safe. It sits between the user
  and pooled data.
- Rollback is a data question. An image that activates newer pool features can
  leave the previous image unable to import those pools. Never propose a ZFS
  line bump without saying that.

## Never weaken a fail-closed check

The build deliberately fails when ZFS does not match the primary kernel, when a
signature cannot be verified, when a digest does not match, or when the akmods
cache does not cover the required kernel and ZFS line. A guard that fires is
reporting a real condition. Fix the cause or stop and report it. Do not relax
it, add a fallback, make it best-effort, or reach for `|| true` — including to
make a test easier to write.

This is the most likely wrong suggestion here, because the guard looks like an
obstacle and the "fix" looks like robustness.

Safety-critical files: build.yml, .github/actions/publish-native-image,
ci_tools/sign_image.py, ci_tools/promote_stable.py,
ci_tools/check_akmods_cache.py,
containerfiles/zfs-akmods/install_zfs_from_akmods_cache.py, and
files/scripts/configure_signing_policy.py. Changing any of them requires an
explicit statement of what could reach a booted machine if the change is wrong.

## Code

- Python 3, standard library only. Nothing here is built, published, or
  installed as a package; ci_tools/ and shared/ run as modules from workflow
  steps. Do not add a dependency.
- `ruff check ci_tools/ shared/ tests/ files/ containerfiles/` must be clean,
  at the version pinned in .github/workflows/test.yml.
- Tests are unittest.TestCase throughout; pytest is only a runner, so
  `python3 -m unittest discover -s tests` works with nothing installed.
- Raise CiToolError for a known refusal. ci_tools/cli.py turns it into exit 1
  with the message on stderr, and every workflow step runs under `set -e`.

## Tests

Never `assertRaises(CiToolError)` on its own — it passes for any reason at all,
including an unrelated missing environment variable, so it reports a guard as
covered when the guard never ran. Pin the message or the specific guard.

Tests under tests/ mock the external calls (registry, cosign, skopeo) the code
under test makes. A per-module coverage floor, where one exists, may be raised
freely; lowering one is a decision with a reason in the commit message.

## Comments

Comments here explain why, especially why something that looks wrong is
deliberate. Several encode a specific past incident. Do not strip or tidy them;
write in the same register.

## Testing reality

The Containerfile and build_files/build-image.sh execute only inside an image
build against a real RPM database and module tree. Nothing on a developer
machine reaches them, so a green local suite is not evidence for a change to
either. State how it was verified, or that it was not.

The image-side Python is different: install_zfs_from_akmods_cache.py and
configure_signing_policy.py have host-side tests with external calls mocked, and
both carry coverage floors. What a green suite does not prove about them is
whatever the mock stood in for.
